[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Divergence found and fixed in Phase B
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **`calculate_subtree_sum` returned the wrong NaN when two addends were both NaN.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Found by row C27 (`wild_values` forests seeded with NaN/inf), then minimised by a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m brute-force sweep over NaN/inf bit patterns.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * The C body is `sum += calculate_subtree_sum(...)`, which gcc compiles to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `addsd %xmm1,%xmm0` where `%xmm0` holds the **recursive result** and `%xmm1`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the accumulator — i.e. the child's value is `ADDSD`'s *destination* operand.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Per Intel SDM Table 4-7, when both operands are NaN `ADDSD` returns the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   destination operand (quieted). So the C propagates the **child's** NaN sign and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   payload.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Rust's `sum += child` puts the accumulator in the destination position, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   because `fadd` is commutative LLVM reorders operands freely — so the Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   returned the **accumulator's** NaN instead. 282 of 1110 minimal combinations
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   disagreed; the sign bit and the payload both differed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Fix: an `addsd(dst, src)` helper in `src/lib.rs` that reproduces the hardware
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rule explicitly (destination NaN wins, quieted; otherwise a plain add). Simply
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   writing `child + sum` was **not** enough — LLVM commuted it straight back.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is only observable through the exported `calculate_subtree_sum`, since
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `maxnmin` funnels every NaN through `safe_double_to_int`, which maps all NaNs to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0`. A `maxnmin`-only test suite would never have found it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Harness bug worth recording
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m An earlier version of the harness shared one `dlopen` handle across test
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m functions. `dlopen` de-duplicates by inode, so all tests in a binary got the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **same** library instance — and therefore the same `node_count` / `node_storage`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m globals. Because cargo runs tests in parallel threads, concurrent `maxnmin` calls
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (which reset `node_count` to 0 mid-flight) corrupted each other and produced four
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m phantom "divergences" in `maxnmin`. Every test now takes a `fresh_pair()`, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `dlopen`s a private per-test copy of each `.so` and thus gets pristine statics.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The lesson is recorded in `tests/common/mod.rs`; there is deliberately no shared
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m loader.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **All 43 rows pass** across the randomized inputs (fixed seeds, reproducible).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/lowlevel_diff.rs` + `tests/nan_propagation.rs` + `tests/maxnmin_diff.rs`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m = 43 test functions, 0 failures.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Independent cross-check (not using the Rust harness)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scripts/xcheck.c` is a standalone C driver that `dlopen`s *one* library and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m prints a transcript: digests over a 390 625-case exhaustive `maxnmin` sweep,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 200 000 random `maxnmin` calls, 200 000 `safe_double_to_int` bit patterns, then a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 60-node forest driven through `add_node` / `find_node_by_id` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `get_children_count` / `calculate_subtree_sum` / `process_string`. Run against
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m each `.so`, its stdout is **byte-identical** (1943 bytes, 170 lines), which rules
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m out the possibility that the Rust harness passes vacuously (e.g. by accidentally
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m loading the C library twice).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Negative control
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scripts/mutation_check.sh` injects 19 known bugs into `src/lib.rs` one at a time
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and confirms the suite fails for each. **19/19 detected.** Two further mutants
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m were investigated and shown to be *behaviourally equivalent* (unobservable
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m through the public API), so their survival is correct, not a gap:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `while i < MAX_NAME_LEN` instead of `MAX_NAME_LEN - 1` in the `strncpy` loop —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   byte 49 is unconditionally overwritten with `'\0'` on the next line either way.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `d >= (double)INT_MAX` instead of `d >` in `safe_double_to_int` — at exactly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `2147483647.0` the clamp returns `INT_MAX` and the fall-through `(int)d` cast
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   also returns `2147483647`.
