[1mOne real translation bug[22m, in [38;5;10mcalculate_subtree_sum[0m, in the NaN-propagation path:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C's [38;5;10msum += calculate_subtree_sum(...)[0m compiles to [38;5;10maddsd %xmm1,%xmm0[0m where the [1mrecursive result[22m sits in the destination register. When both addends are NaN, x86 [38;5;10mADDSD[0m returns the [3mdestination[23m operand (quieted), so the C propagates the [1mchild's[22m NaN sign and payload. Rust's [38;5;10msum += child[0m has the accumulator in the destination position — and since [38;5;10mfadd[0m is commutative, LLVM freely reorders operands, so the Rust returned the accumulator's NaN instead. 282 of 1110 minimal bit-pattern combinations disagreed.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Rewriting it as [38;5;10mchild + sum[0m did [1mnot[22m fix it (LLVM commuted it straight back); the fix is an explicit [38;5;10maddsd()[0m helper reproducing the hardware rule. This is only observable through the exported [38;5;10mcalculate_subtree_sum[0m — [38;5;10mmaxnmin[0m funnels every NaN through [38;5;10msafe_double_to_int[0m, which maps all NaNs to [38;5;10m0[0m. A test suite driving only the top-level wrapper would never have found it.[0m[0m
